# Firebase
# The service-account private key grants ADMIN access to the Firebase project
# (it can send a push to any registered device). It is uploaded to EAS
# credentials and must NEVER be committed.
*firebase-adminsdk*.json
serviceAccountKey.json

# Google Play submission
# The Play service-account key lets anyone upload releases to your listing.
# Referenced by eas.json -> submit.production.android.serviceAccountKeyPath.
play-service-account.json

# App signing credentials pulled down from EAS
# `eas credentials` -> Credentials JSON -> Download writes BOTH of these:
# credentials.json holds the keystore password, key alias and key password in
# PLAINTEXT, and credentials/ holds the .jks itself. Anyone with the pair can
# sign a build that Play accepts as an update to this app. *.jks above covers
# the keystore; these two cover the passwords and the folder around it.
credentials.json
credentials/
# The base64 transcription made when pasting the keystore into a GitHub secret.
# Same private key, just text — and text is exactly what `git add .` sweeps up.
# Delete it once the secret is saved; this is the belt to that braces.
keystore.b64
*.jks.b64
# NOTE: google-services.json is deliberately NOT ignored. It is not a secret —
# it ships inside the APK — and EAS cloud builds skip gitignored files, so the
# build needs it committed.

# Supabase CLI local state (link target, temp files) — machine-specific
supabase/.temp/
